
Diagnosed permission deficiency and prescribed elevated API token privileges

This one's on the Proxmox permissions side, not Terraform — good sign that it's now actually talking to the API.

The query-url-metadata/download-url endpoints Proxmox uses to fetch a file by URL require Sys.Audit and Sys.Modify privileges at the node/root path — separate from whatever VM/storage permissions your terraform-prov@pam token already has for creating VMs. That's a well-known gap: minimal tokens set up just for VM management usually don't have it, so the API call gets rejected with 403 even though the token works fine for everything else.

Fix in the Proxmox UI:

    Datacenter → Permissions → Add → User Permission
    Path: /
    User: terraform-prov@pam (the token's parent user, not the token itself)
    Role: either PVEAdmin (simplest, broad), or if you want to stay minimal, create/use a custom role that includes Sys.Audit, Sys.Modify, Datastore.AllocateSpace, Datastore.Audit
    Save, then rerun terraform apply

If you'd rather do it via CLI on the Proxmox host instead of the UI:
bash

pveum acl modify / --users terraform-prov@pam --roles PVEAdmin

Once that permission's in place this should get past the download and move on to actually creating the VMs — that's the real long pole in this apply.

That's the likely culprit — API tokens in Proxmox default to "Privilege Separation" enabled, meaning the token (terraform-prov@pam!automation) does not automatically inherit the permissions you just granted the user (terraform-prov@pam). It's treated as a separate identity in the ACL system. So the token creating your other VMs is likely still running on whatever narrower role it had before.

Two ways to fix it:

Option A — grant the permission to the token itself (keeps privilege separation, more explicit):
bash

pveum acl modify / --tokens 'terraform-prov@pam!automation' --roles PVEAdmin

Note the --tokens flag (not --users) and the token is referenced as user@realm!tokenid.

Option B — disable privilege separation on the token so it just inherits whatever the user has (simpler, less isolated):

    Datacenter → Permissions → API Tokens → find terraform-prov@pam!automation → uncheck "Privilege Separation" → Save.

Either works; Option A is the more correct long-term habit since it keeps the token scoped independently of the user account. Rerun terraform apply after either change.

terraform output -raw kubeconfig > kubeconfig
export KUBECONFIG=$PWD/kubeconfig